  "term": "vocal_formant",
  "says": "A voice-like tone: a harmonically rich source (a saw or a pulse, the glottal pulse's stand-in) shaped by a bank of resonances at the vowel's formants - for 'a' about 700 and 1100 Hz, for 'i' about 300 and 1900 Hz - and made to talk by moving between vowels slowly, with a little vibrato. In this engine the formant filter (model 5) holds four such resonances per vowel on an X/Y pad (style 0: a, i, u, o at the corners); an LFO under a hertz on formant_x morphs between vowels, formant_resonance sets how peaked they are. Signature: a harmonic tone (the source is periodic) whose centroid sits in the vowel region and moves by a few semitones at the morph's rate, with nothing harsh above the formants. Measured 2026-09-14: a raw saw into the formant bank was refuted on harshness (-5.9 dB: the saw's -6 dB/octave leaves the upper formants at 2.6-3.2 kHz too much to work with, where a glottal pulse falls at -12); a 12 dB low-pass at 1.3 kHz after the bank stands in for that tilt and the entry validates at -25.5 dB, centroid 650 Hz, brightness moving 4.1 semitones at the morph.",
  "sources": [
